EXT. STREET - DAY

Bright afternoon sun paints the sidewalk in warm yellow light.

An eight-year-old South Asian girl walks with purpose. This is AANYA.

She wears a mustard yellow sweater. The sleeves are pulled over her hands. She wears gray leggings.

Around her neck hang blue noise-reducing headphones.

Aanya is an autistic girl. She notices sequence and rhythm in everything.

She holds a small notebook. It is her Quiet Book.

On the page, she has drawn a map of the neighborhood. Blue circles mark the quietest places.

Aanya reaches into her pocket. She turns a smooth gray pebble with her fingers.

Her MOM walks beside her, carrying a grocery bag.

MOM
We need to stop by the market first, Aanya.

AANYA
The market has high-frequency hums from the freezers. It is a level-four noise zone.

MOM
Just five minutes. I promise.

Aanya looks at her map. She points to a blank green space.

AANYA
I must find the zero-decibel spot in Elm Creek Park today. Tomorrow they turn on the new splash pad. Then the quiet will be gone.

MOM
I know how much that quiet spot means to you. We will get there.

They reach the intersection of Oak Street and Main Street.

EXT. OAK STREET CROSSWALK - DAY

Cars idle at the red light. The engine noises rumble.

Aanya pulls her headphones over her ears. The rumble softens.

She looks up at the pedestrian crossing signal.

The orange hand is flashing.

Flash. Flash. Pause. Flash.

It is not a steady blink.

Aanya stops. She tilts her head.

She counts the seconds between the flashes.

One, two, three. Flash.

One, two. Flash.

One. Flash.

She pulls her headphones down to her neck.

AANYA
Mom. Look at the signal.

MOM
It is just telling us to wait, sweetie.

AANYA
No. It is counting down. The interval is shrinking by one second every cycle.

Mom looks at her phone. She does not see the pattern.

MOM
It is probably just an old bulb, Aanya. Let us cross.

The light turns green. They walk across the street.

EXT. PARK ROAD - DAY

Aanya keeps her eyes on the next traffic light down the block.

This light is also flashing. It flashes faster.

Flash-flash. Pause. Flash-flash.

Aanya stops on the sidewalk. She pulls her Quiet Book out.

She draws a line connecting the traffic lights on her map.

AANYA
It is not a loose bulb. It is a sequence. The lights are talking to each other.

A boy from her school, LEO (8), rides up on a blue bicycle. He stops near them.

LEO
Hi, Aanya. What are you drawing?

AANYA
I am mapping the signal pulse. Look.

She points to the traffic light at the corner of Park Road.

It flashes three times quickly, then stops for half a second.

LEO
Whoa. That looks like a video game glitch.

AANYA
It is a grid overload sequence. The solar panels on the library roof are sending too much power to the streetlights.

Leo blinks. He looks at the light, then at Aanya.

LEO
How do you know that?

AANYA
Because the library panels pulse in groups of three when they are full. The traffic lights are trying to dump the extra energy. But they cannot.

MOM
Are you sure, Aanya? That seems like a big jump.

AANYA
My brain tracks the patterns. The pattern never lies.

EXT. ELM CREEK PARK - DAY

They enter the park. The grass is green. Large oak trees shade the paths.

In the center of the park stands a gray metal electrical transformer box.

It is labeled: MAIN GRID CONTROL - ZONE 4.

Nearby, a construction crew is assembling the new splash pad. Noisy metal pipes clank together.

Aanya winces at the clanking. She touches the smooth gray pebble in her pocket to ground herself.

She walks toward the transformer box. A small red warning light on the side is blinking.

Blink-blink-blink. Pause. Blink-blink-blink.

OFFICER DAVIS, a friendly community crossing guard, stands near the park entrance.

Aanya walks straight up to him.

AANYA
Officer Davis. The grid is going to blow.

OFFICER DAVIS
Hello, Aanya. What makes you say that?

AANYA
The traffic lights on Main Street are dumping power here. The countdown is at level two. When it reaches zero, the transformer will burst. It will make a very loud noise.

Officer Davis scratches his head. He pats his radio.

OFFICER DAVIS
The city engineers check these boxes every week, kiddo. It is probably just a system update.

AANYA
An update has a steady blue light. This is a flashing red danger code. The intervals are down to half a second.

Officer Davis looks at the box. He sees the red light flashing rapidly.

OFFICER DAVIS
Well, I am sure it is fine. But I can call it in later.

AANYA
Later is too late. The countdown will hit zero in four minutes.

Officer Davis looks skeptical. He does not move.

Aanya turns to Leo.

AANYA
He does not see the sequence. But we do.

LEO
What do we do?

AANYA
We have to manual-bypass the transformer. We need to route the extra power to the park's backup battery.

LEO
How?

Aanya approaches the gray metal box. She looks at the interface panel.

There is a small keypad with four colored buttons: Yellow, Blue, Green, and Red.

Above the keypad, the red warning light flashes at a blinding speed.

It is almost a solid glare now.

EXT. GRID CONTROL BOX - CONTINUOUS

Aanya stares at the flashing red light.

Her eyes track the tiny pauses between the bright flashes.

She hears the construction noise behind her. Clank! Clank!

It is getting harder to focus.

She pulls her blue headphones over her ears. The world goes quiet.

She can only hear her own breathing. And she can see the light.

Inside her mind, the flashes turn into numbers.

Three short. Two long. One short.

She looks at the colored buttons.

Yellow is short. Blue is long. Green is neutral.

AANYA
(to herself)
Yellow, yellow, yellow. Blue, blue. Yellow.

She reaches out her hand. Her mustard yellow sleeve covers her wrist.

She presses the Yellow button three times.

Click. Click. Click.

She presses the Blue button twice.

Click. Click.

She presses the Yellow button once.

Click.

An alarm on the box stops buzzing. The red warning light turns a solid, calm green.

A low, gentle hum replaces the angry clicking sound.

Across the street, the traffic lights stop flashing. They return to their normal, steady cycles.

Officer Davis stares at the box in surprise.

OFFICER DAVIS
My goodness. The warning light actually stopped.

Leo cheers.

LEO
That was amazing, Aanya! You totally figured it out!

Aanya slides her headphones down to her neck. She smiles a small, satisfied smile.

AANYA
It was just a simple sequence. The grid needed to be told where to put the extra energy.

Mom walks over and hugs Aanya gently.

MOM
I am so proud of you, Aanya. You saved the park from a very loud afternoon.

AANYA
And I saved my quiet spot.

EXT. ELM CREEK PARK - DEEP WOODS - LATER

Aanya walks past the construction area, deep into the quietest corner of the park.

Here, the tall oak trees block out the city sounds.

The air is cool and peaceful.

She sits down on a smooth wooden bench.

She opens her Quiet Book.

With a blue crayon, she draws a perfect, solid blue circle around this spot.

She writes: THE ZERO-DECIBEL ZONE.

Leo and Mom sit on the grass nearby, giving her space to enjoy her quiet.

Aanya reaches into her pocket. She pulls out her smooth gray pebble.

She holds it up to the sunlight, feeling its cool, solid weight.

She is an autistic girl, and she knows exactly how to read the world.

She closes her book and smiles into the quiet.

THE END